/* Track: Saturation and queueing. Ordered foundational first. */
(window.LESSONS = window.LESSONS || []).push(
{
  id: "saturation-littles-law",
  track: "saturation", level: "mechanism",
  title: "Little's law tells you the pool size your latency budget already implies",
  source: "Mor Harchol-Balter, Performance Modeling and Design of Computer Systems: Queueing Theory in Action, Cambridge University Press, 2013",
  gateIntro: "A Go service in front of Postgres plateaus at a throughput number nobody chose. Adding pods does not move it. CPU on the pods sits under a third and the database reports low CPU throughout. As offered load rises past the plateau, p99 climbs smoothly while completed requests per second stays flat. The plateau has been at the same number for months, across three unrelated deploys.",
  idea: "Concurrency equals arrival rate times residence time, so a pool size and a latency target together fix the maximum throughput, and whichever of the three you did not choose has been chosen for you by accident.",
  why: "Little's law says the mean number of items in a system equals the mean arrival rate times the mean time each spends there. It is distribution-free: no assumption about the arrival process, the service time distribution, or the scheduling discipline, only that the system is stable over the interval you measure. That is what makes it usable at 2am. A pool of 50 connections with a mean residence time of 100 milliseconds absorbs 500 requests per second and not one more, regardless of what the capacity plan says.\n\nThe operational consequence is that the three numbers are jointly determined and are usually set by three different people who have never spoken. The latency target lives in an SLO document. The pool size lives in a Helm values file. The arrival rate is whatever the tenants do. Worse, residence time includes queue time, so as you approach the ceiling the residence term grows and the arrival rate the pool can absorb actually falls. The ceiling is not a wall you hit, it is a wall that moves towards you.",
  failureMode: "A Temporal worker with MaxConcurrentActivityExecutionSize of 100 and an activity that averages two seconds absorbs 50 activity executions per second. A tenant schedules a backfill producing several hundred per second. The backlog grows and nobody notices, because worker CPU sits at 30 percent: the workers are blocked on a downstream call, not on the CPU. The metric that would have caught it is schedule-to-start latency, and nobody had a panel for it.",
  experiment: "For the three busiest pools you run - connection pools, worker concurrency limits, semaphores - write down the configured concurrency and measure mean residence time over the last hour. Divide concurrency by residence time to get the throughput ceiling. Compare that ceiling with the peak arrival rate over the same hour. Any pool whose ratio is under two has no burst headroom, and you now know its number.",
  reflection: "Of the three numbers - concurrency, residence time, arrival rate - which was last chosen deliberately in your estate, and who chose it?",
  recall: {
    q: "A service has a fixed worker pool and its throughput has stopped rising with load, while CPU on the workers is low. Without profiling anything, what two numbers do you need to state the ceiling, and why does low CPU not contradict the ceiling?",
    a: "Concurrency and mean residence time. Their quotient is the throughput ceiling, and residence time counts the whole time an item occupies a slot, including time blocked on a downstream call.\n\nLow CPU is consistent with a saturated pool because the constraint is occupancy of slots, not consumption of cycles. A worker blocked on I/O holds its slot and burns no CPU, so the CPU graph is measuring the wrong resource.",
  },
  deepDive: "Take my Temporal worker configuration and my activity duration histograms and tell me which task queue reaches its Little's law ceiling first at current growth."
},
{
  id: "saturation-variability-knee",
  track: "saturation", level: "mechanism",
  title: "Queueing delay is set by variability as much as by utilisation",
  source: "J. F. C. Kingman, The Single Server Queue in Heavy Traffic, 1961; Mor Harchol-Balter, Performance Modeling and Design of Computer Systems, 2013",
  gateIntro: "A shared worker fleet runs at about half its configured concurrency on a one-minute average. Queue wait for the median task is under a second. Queue wait at p99 is eleven minutes and has been for three weeks. Doubling the fleet moved the p99 by less than a third. The tenants complaining are not the tenants submitting the most tasks.",
  idea: "Queueing delay is the utilisation term rho over one minus rho, multiplied by the variability of arrivals and service times, so a bursty or heavy-tailed workload puts the knee far below the utilisation you planned for.",
  why: "Kingman's approximation for a general single-server queue factors the mean wait into three parts: a utilisation term, rho over one minus rho; a variability term, the mean of the squared coefficients of variation of interarrival and service times; and a scale term, the mean service time. Everyone knows the first factor. The second is a straight multiplier and it is the one that ruins capacity plans. With Poisson arrivals and exponential service both coefficients are one and the multiplier is one. Make service times heavy-tailed with a coefficient of variation of three and the multiplier is five, so a queue at 50 percent utilisation waits as long as a well-behaved queue at 83 percent.\n\nThis is why 'we run everything at 70 percent' is not a policy, it is a number borrowed from a system with different variability. It also tells you the cheapest lever. You can buy utilisation headroom with hardware, or you can buy variability reduction by splitting one queue into size classes, capping the largest permitted unit of work, or smoothing admissions. The second is usually free and moves the same term.",
  failureMode: "One shared task queue where most workflows finish in seconds and one tenant's full metadata crawl runs for forty minutes. Fleet utilisation on a one-minute average is comfortable, so nothing alerts. Schedule-to-start p99 for every other tenant is minutes, because the coefficient of variation of service times on that queue is enormous. Adding workers helps sublinearly, because the variability multiplier is untouched by the extra capacity.",
  experiment: "Pick one shared worker queue. Pull the last week of task durations and compute the coefficient of variation: standard deviation divided by mean. Above two and your queueing delay is dominated by spread rather than by load, and the correct intervention is to split the queue by duration class. Then compute the same figure for interarrival times to see whether the burstiness is on the input side instead.",
  reflection: "Which of your shared queues has the widest service time spread, and what would the size classes be if you split it tomorrow?",
  recall: {
    q: "Two worker fleets both run at 50 percent utilisation. One has p99 queue wait in milliseconds, the other in minutes. What single measurement distinguishes them and what does it imply about the fix?",
    a: "The coefficient of variation of service times, and of interarrival times. Kingman's approximation multiplies the utilisation term by the mean of their squares, so two systems at identical utilisation can differ by an order of magnitude in wait.\n\nThe implication is that adding capacity moves only the utilisation factor, while splitting the queue by size class or capping the largest work unit moves the variability factor. When variability is the dominant term, capacity is the expensive way to buy the same result.",
  },
  deepDive: "Compute the coefficient of variation of task durations per Temporal task queue from my ClickHouse history and rank the queues by how far the variability multiplier has pulled the knee to the left."
},
{
  id: "saturation-pool-is-the-queue",
  track: "saturation", level: "mechanism",
  title: "The connection pool is the queue, and a small pool fails better than a large one",
  source: "Brett Wooldridge, About Pool Sizing, HikariCP wiki",
  gateIntro: "A team raises a service's database connection pool after seeing acquisition timeouts. The timeouts stop within minutes. Over the next hour median query latency roughly doubles and p99 goes up by more, while database CPU is unchanged. Two hours later readiness probes on the service begin failing intermittently and pods start cycling.",
  idea: "A pool sized larger than the downstream can serve does not add capacity, it relocates the queue to somewhere you cannot see it or shed from it.",
  why: "The Oracle Real-World Performance work that Wooldridge cites shows throughput on a fixed-core database rising when connection counts are reduced. Past the point where the cores are busy, extra concurrent sessions buy context switching, lock contention and cache thrash, not work. HikariCP's guidance is a small pool sized against the downstream's actual parallelism, not against the application's peak concurrency. A database with eight cores does not do more work with two hundred connections than with twenty; it does the same work with more contention and longer per-query latency.\n\nThe operational argument is stronger than the throughput one. A small pool keeps the queue explicit and local. Acquisition timeout is a knob you own, it fires inside your process, it produces a typed error you can map to a 503, and it lets you refuse by tenant before the work starts. Once the concurrency is inside the database, your only shedding primitive is killing sessions, and you have lost the ability to distinguish a request that queued from a request that ran slowly.",
  failureMode: "Pool raised from 20 to 200 across 15 replicas puts 3000 potential sessions against a database with 16 cores. Acquisition timeouts vanish, which is read as a fix. What actually happened is that the fast local failure was replaced by a slow remote one: everything is now slower, including the health check query, so Kubernetes removes pods and concentrates the same load on fewer replicas.",
  experiment: "Take one service's pool size, multiply by replica count, and compare against the database's core count and max_connections. More than roughly two connections per database core and you are queueing inside the database. Then grep your logs for the pool acquisition timeout error: if it is not a distinct, countable error code, you currently cannot tell the two queues apart during an incident.",
  reflection: "Where in your estate is the pool size larger than the downstream's parallelism, and what would break if you halved it on Monday?",
  recall: {
    q: "Acquisition timeouts on a connection pool are firing. Why is raising the pool size usually the wrong first move, and what does it change about the failure you will see next?",
    a: "The pool timeout is the system telling you the downstream cannot serve the offered concurrency. Raising the pool does not increase the downstream's parallelism, it lets more work in to contend, so throughput is flat or worse and per-query latency rises.\n\nThe failure changes from a fast, local, typed error you can shed on, into a diffuse slowdown inside the database that also affects health checks and admin queries. You have traded a legible failure at a boundary you control for an illegible one you do not.",
  },
  deepDive: "Given my services' pool sizes, replica counts and the database core counts, tell me which pools are oversized against the downstream and what acquisition timeout I should set instead."
},
{
  id: "saturation-kinesis-thread-limit",
  track: "saturation", level: "amplifier",
  title: "AWS Kinesis, 25 November 2020: adding capacity crossed a per-server limit that scaled with fleet size",
  source: "AWS, Summary of the Amazon Kinesis Event in the Northern Virginia (US-EAST-1) Region, November 2020",
  gateIntro: "On 25 November 2020 an AWS team added capacity to the front-end fleet of Kinesis Data Streams in us-east-1. Errors on Kinesis APIs began at around 5:15 AM PST. Dependent services followed: CloudWatch metrics, Cognito, and other services that use Kinesis internally. Because the tooling behind the public status dashboard itself depended on affected services, status updates lagged the outage. Removing the newly added capacity did not restore service quickly, and Kinesis was not fully recovered until late that evening.",
  idea: "Each front-end server maintained operating system threads in proportion to the number of other servers in the fleet, so a routine capacity addition pushed every server in the fleet past a per-server limit at the same instant.",
  why: "The Kinesis front-end fleet builds a shard-map by exchanging information with every other front-end server, and each server carried threads for its peers. Total threads per server therefore scaled with fleet size. Adding servers raised the per-server thread count everywhere at once, and because every server ran the same configuration, they all crossed the operating system limit simultaneously. The resource that ran out was not the resource being added, and the failure was perfectly correlated across the fleet rather than sampled across it.\n\nThe recovery was slow for a related reason. Restarting a front-end server requires it to rebuild the shard-map by contacting every peer, which is slow, and restarting many at once would have overwhelmed the metadata path. So capacity had to be returned in small increments over hours with verification between them. A limit that grows with fleet size makes both the failure and the repair superlinear in the size of your success, and it converts your growth into a scheduled outage at an unknown future date.",
  failureMode: "Any all-to-all structure sized against today's fleet: a gossip mesh, one gRPC connection or goroutine per peer, one informer or watch per controller replica against the Kubernetes API server, per-peer file descriptors. It works, and keeps working, until a scale-out or a node pool resize crosses the limit for every replica at once, which is exactly when you are least able to reason about it.",
  experiment: "Enumerate every per-node resource whose count is a function of node count: peers, connections, watches, threads, file descriptors. For the largest one, compute the value at three times your current fleet size and compare against the real limit - ulimit -n, threads-max, the client library's connection cap, the API server's watch budget. Write down the ratio for each. Anything under three is a scale-out away from firing.",
  reflection: "Which limit in your estate grows with the number of nodes, and what is the node count at which it fires?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why did adding capacity to a fleet take down the whole fleet rather than just the new servers, and why did removing that capacity not fix it?",
    a: "Each server held threads proportional to the number of peers, so the added servers raised the per-server thread count on every existing server too. All servers shared a configuration and crossed the operating system limit together, which made the failure fleet-wide and simultaneous rather than partial.\n\nRemoval did not fix it because the servers were already in a bad state and needed restarting, and each restart required rebuilding a shard-map by contacting every peer. That rebuild is slow and cannot be parallelised without overwhelming the metadata path, so recovery was throttled by the same all-to-all structure that caused the failure.",
  },
  deepDive: "Walk my Kubernetes controllers, Temporal workers and service mesh for anything whose per-node resource usage scales with node count, and tell me the fleet size at which each hits its limit."
},
{
  id: "saturation-coordinated-omission",
  track: "saturation", level: "mechanism",
  title: "Your latency histogram omits exactly the requests you care about",
  source: "Gil Tene, How NOT to Measure Latency",
  gateIntro: "A load test reports p99 of 40 milliseconds and a maximum under a second, computed over millions of samples, and it reproduces cleanly on every run. Production, at a lower average request rate, has support tickets describing stalls of several seconds a few times an hour. The load generator drives the same client library the production callers use, against the same build.",
  idea: "A load generator that waits for a response before sending the next request stops sending requests precisely when the system is stalled, so the samples that would have recorded the stall are never taken.",
  why: "In an open model, requests arrive whether or not the system is ready to serve them. A closed-loop generator with N threads has at most N requests in flight, so a one-second stall produces N slow samples instead of the thousands of requests a real arrival process would have queued during that second. The tail is therefore measured with a sample size that shrinks exactly as latency rises. Tene's name for it is coordinated omission: the measuring apparatus has quietly coordinated with the system under test to skip the interesting part.\n\nThe magnitude is not subtle. A system that answers in one millisecond for almost the whole run and stalls for ten seconds once will, under a closed loop, contribute a single ten-second sample among millions and report a p99.99 of about one millisecond. Under an open model, every request that should have been issued during those ten seconds carries its residual wait, and the honest p99.99 is in seconds. The correction is to record against intended start time rather than actual, which is what HdrHistogram's recordValueWithExpectedInterval does, or to run an open-model generator that does not throttle itself when the system stalls.",
  failureMode: "The same bug lives in your own instrumentation, not just in the load tool. A handler that starts its timer after the request has been dequeued from the accept queue, pulled off a channel, or granted a semaphore is measuring service time and calling it latency. Under saturation, service time is the one component that does not move, so the histogram stays flat while users wait, and the graph becomes actively misleading at exactly the moment you need it.",
  experiment: "Take one latency histogram you rely on and find the line where the timer starts. If it starts after the work was admitted, it excludes queue time. Add a second timer started at connection accept or at message enqueue and run both for an hour of real traffic. Compare the two p99s. The gap is the part of your latency you have never measured.",
  reflection: "Which of your dashboards measures service time while claiming to measure latency, and what did the gap turn out to be?",
  recall: {
    q: "A closed-loop load test reports an excellent p99.99 over ten million samples. Why can that number be wrong by three orders of magnitude, and what would you change to get an honest one?",
    a: "A closed-loop generator only issues a request after the previous one returns, so during a stall it issues almost nothing. The stall produces a handful of long samples rather than the many thousands an open arrival process would have accumulated, and the percentile is computed over a sample that systematically under-represents the bad minutes.\n\nFix it either by running an open-model generator that issues at a fixed rate regardless of responses, or by recording against intended send time and backfilling the omitted intervals, which is what HdrHistogram's expected-interval recording does.",
  },
  deepDive: "Audit where my Go and Python services start their latency timers and tell me which histograms exclude queue or admission time."
},
{
  id: "saturation-bufferbloat",
  track: "saturation", level: "mechanism",
  title: "A queue that is too long converts loss into latency and hides the overload",
  source: "Jim Gettys and Kathleen Nichols, Bufferbloat: Dark Buffers in the Internet, ACM Queue, 2011",
  gateIntro: "Users on a home link report that interactive sessions become unusable while a large upload runs. Round-trip times to the first hop rise from tens of milliseconds to several seconds during the transfer and return to normal the moment it finishes. Packet loss on the link is zero throughout. Replacing the router with a newer, faster model made the effect worse.",
  idea: "A buffer larger than the control loop's reaction time delays the backpressure signal until the sender has already committed far more work than the system can complete.",
  why: "Loss-based congestion control infers path capacity from drops. Buffers exist so a short burst is absorbed rather than dropped, which is correct up to about a round trip's worth of data. Beyond that, the buffer is storing the signal the sender needs rather than delivering it, so the sender keeps increasing its rate into a queue that is already full, and everything sharing the link inherits a standing multi-second delay. Memory got cheap faster than the algorithms were revised, so vendors shipped ever deeper buffers and the internet acquired seconds of latency on links that never lost a packet. Gettys and Nichols' remedy is active queue management: drop or mark early and deliberately, rather than storing.\n\nThe structure generalises exactly. Every work queue between your services is a buffer, and its depth in seconds is the delay you have inserted into your own feedback loop. A Kafka topic, an SQS queue, a Temporal task queue, an in-process channel with a generous capacity: each one delays the moment the producer learns the consumer cannot keep up. The right way to size a buffer is in units of time - the burst duration you intend to absorb - not in units of memory you happen to have.",
  failureMode: "A Temporal task queue accepts a tenant's two hundred thousand workflow backfill without complaint. The producing job finishes in minutes and reports success. Consumers drain for eleven hours, every other workflow on that queue waits behind it, and the only signal available at the moment of the decision was that the enqueue returned 200.",
  experiment: "For every queue between your services, compute drain time when full: capacity divided by current consumer throughput. Express it in minutes. Any queue whose full-drain time exceeds your incident response time is storing an outage rather than absorbing a burst. Write the list, sorted descending, and pick the top one to bound.",
  reflection: "Which of your queues has the longest full-drain time, and what should its bound be expressed in seconds of burst?",
  recall: {
    q: "A queue between two services was made deeper to stop producers seeing errors during bursts, and errors stopped. What did that change actually do to the system's behaviour under sustained overload?",
    a: "It converted rejection into delay and postponed the feedback. The producer now learns nothing about consumer capacity, so it continues committing work at a rate the consumer cannot sustain, and the backlog grows until something else breaks - memory, a deadline, or a downstream timeout.\n\nThe correct sizing is the burst duration you intend to absorb, measured in seconds of consumer throughput. Anything deeper is a latency commitment you have made without deciding to.",
  },
  deepDive: "List every queue in my Argo to Temporal path with its bound and current consumer rate, and tell me which ones have a drain time longer than my on-call response time."
},
{
  id: "saturation-head-of-line",
  track: "saturation", level: "mechanism",
  title: "One slow item at the head stops everything behind it",
  source: "Daniel Stenberg, HTTP/3 explained (head-of-line blocking)",
  gateIntro: "After a service moves from many parallel HTTP/1.1 connections to a single multiplexed HTTP/2 connection, mean latency improves and p99 gets worse. On lossy mobile networks the effect is severe: a client losing a small fraction of packets sees all of its concurrent requests stall together, and the stalls are tightly correlated in time across requests that share nothing at the application layer.",
  idea: "Multiplexing many logical streams onto one ordered channel makes the slowest or most delayed unit set the completion time for every unit behind it.",
  why: "HTTP/2 multiplexes streams over a single TCP connection, and TCP delivers bytes in order. A lost segment therefore holds back every stream whose bytes arrive after it, including streams whose own data arrived intact and is sitting in the receiver's buffer. The application sees many independent requests; the transport sees one ordered byte stream. That mismatch is the whole of head-of-line blocking, and it is the reason HTTP/3 moved to QUIC, which keeps ordering per stream so an unaffected stream can be delivered while another retransmits.\n\nThe same shape recurs above the transport wherever independent work shares a serialisation point. A single-partition Kafka topic. One ordered task queue serving all tenants. A worker pool polling FIFO. A mutex held across an I/O call. In each case the ordering constraint, not the capacity, is the coupling: work that has no logical dependency acquires one. The fix is always to partition by whatever makes the coupling match the real dependency - per-tenant queues, per-key partitions, per-stream ordering - and never to add capacity behind the same single channel.",
  failureMode: "One Temporal task queue for all tenants with workers polling in order. A tenant enqueues five thousand long-running activities. Every other tenant's short activities sit behind them. Worker CPU is healthy, the only moving signal is queue depth, and the affected tenants have done nothing at all. The mitigation available mid-incident is to find and pause one tenant, which takes longer than the incident should last.",
  experiment: "List the serialisation points in one request path: single-partition topics, single ordered queues, global mutexes, single multiplexed connections. For each, multiply the slowest unit of work that can occupy it by how many such units can queue ahead of an arbitrary request. That product is the worst-case wait an unrelated request inherits. Write down the largest one.",
  reflection: "Which serialisation point in your estate couples tenants who share nothing, and what is the partition key that would decouple them?",
  recall: {
    q: "Moving to a single multiplexed connection lowered mean latency and raised p99. Name the mechanism and say why adding bandwidth would not help.",
    a: "Head-of-line blocking. The streams are independent at the application layer but share one ordered delivery channel, so a delayed or lost unit at the head holds back every stream behind it regardless of their own state.",
  },
  deepDive: "Find the serialisation points shared across tenants in my Temporal and Kafka topology and propose partition keys that would decouple them."
},
{
  id: "saturation-backpressure",
  track: "saturation", level: "mechanism",
  title: "Backpressure is the only alternative to unbounded queueing",
  source: "Zach Tellman, Everything Will Flow, 2017",
  gateIntro: "A pipeline ingests tenant events, transforms them, and writes to a store. It keeps up for a week. Then the store's write latency roughly doubles for reasons of its own. Ingest continues to return success and the producer's dashboards look unchanged. Twenty minutes later the transformer pods are OOMKilled in a rolling pattern, restart, and are killed again.",
  idea: "Every unbounded queue is a decision to fail later and less legibly, so the design question is never whether to reject work but where the rejection becomes visible to the caller.",
  why: "Tellman's framing is a dichotomy with no third branch. A queue is either bounded, in which case you have chosen what happens when it is full, or it is unbounded, in which case memory is the bound and the chosen behaviour is an out-of-memory kill with no attribution, no retry-after, and no indication of which caller caused it. Latency in a queueing system is capacity over throughput, so an unbounded queue is an unbounded latency commitment, and a request served after its caller has given up has negative value: it consumed capacity that a live request needed.\n\nBackpressure is the propagation of 'not now' upstream, and the only interesting question is where it surfaces. At the API boundary it is a 429 with a retry-after, and the caller can decide to slow down, queue locally, or tell a human. Three hops in it is an OOM, which reaches the caller minutes later as a 500 and is indistinguishable from a transient fault, so the caller retries and adds load. The bound has to exist somewhere. Putting it at the edge is the only place where the rejection carries enough context to be acted on.",
  failureMode: "A Python consumer reads from Kafka into an in-memory list, batches, and writes downstream. When the downstream slows, the list grows, and the consumer keeps committing offsets because it has accepted the messages. The pod is killed, the uncommitted work is replayed, the replay is larger than the steady-state rate, and the second kill arrives faster than the first.",
  experiment: "Grep one service for unbounded accumulation: make(chan T, N) with a large N, slices or lists appended to inside a stream loop, any executor whose work queue length is not exported as a metric. For each one, write the sentence 'if the consumer runs 10x slower for ten minutes, then ...'. Wherever the sentence ends in 'the pod is killed', that OOM is your current admission control policy.",
  reflection: "Where does your pipeline currently express 'not now', and how many hops from the caller does that happen?",
  recall: {
    q: "A colleague argues that bounding a queue just moves the failure earlier and makes users see errors they would not otherwise see. What is the counterargument?",
    a: "The queue is bounded either way - by an explicit limit you chose or by available memory. The unbounded version does not avoid the error, it defers it until the process dies, at which point the failure is untyped, unattributed, affects in-flight work that would otherwise have succeeded, and reaches the caller as a generic 500 that invites a retry.\n\nA bounded queue converts that into a 429 at a boundary where the caller's identity, priority and retry policy are all known, and where the rejection is cheap.",
  },
  deepDive: "Audit my ingestion path for unbounded queues and tell me where to put the bound so the rejection surfaces at the tenant-facing API rather than as an OOM."
},
{
  id: "saturation-admission-control",
  track: "saturation", level: "mechanism",
  title: "Admission control decides which requests to serve before the queue decides for you",
  source: "Zhou et al., Overload Control for Scaling WeChat Microservices, SoCC 2018 (DAGOR)",
  gateIntro: "A large microservice estate absorbs a traffic surge. Each service independently begins refusing a portion of what it cannot handle, and no individual service reports refusing more than a modest fraction. End-to-end success rate falls far further than any single service's refusal rate would suggest, and the requests that do complete return partial results. Adding capacity to the most-loaded service barely moves the end-to-end number.",
  idea: "Independent admission decisions at every hop multiply, so priority has to be assigned once at the entry point and carried down with the request.",
  why: "DAGOR does three things that uncoordinated rate limiters do not. It detects overload from the average queuing time of requests waiting in a service's own pending queue rather than from response time, because response time includes downstream latency and therefore blames the wrong service. It assigns each request two priorities at the entry point - a business priority from the type of action, and a user priority derived by hashing the user identifier so it is stable for a period - and it propagates both downstream. And it makes admission collaborative: a service that has narrowed its admitted priority range tells its callers, so upstream stops sending work that will be discarded after they have already paid for it.\n\nThe multiplication is the whole argument. A request touching five services where each independently admits 80 percent completes with probability 0.8 to the fifth, about a third, and the two thirds that failed consumed real work at every hop before failing. Coherent shedding turns that into most requests completing fully and the rest refused at the door for almost nothing. The user-priority hash matters as much: uniform random shedding gives every user a broken experience, while shedding by user gives most users a working one and a minority a clear failure.",
  failureMode: "Every service has its own rate limiter tuned by a different team against a different metric, and no shared notion of what a request is worth. Under load, a tenant's interactive UI request is dropped at the fifth hop after the expensive lineage expansion at hops one to three has already been paid for, while a low-value background reindex at hop one is admitted because that hop was not the bottleneck.",
  experiment: "Trace one end-to-end user action through your services and count the independent admission decisions on the path: rate limiters, semaphores, pool acquisition timeouts, circuit breakers, queue bounds. Multiply the admission probabilities each would apply at twice current load. Then check the last hop for whether any tenant identifier or priority from the entry point is still present in the request context. If it is not, coherent shedding is currently impossible.",
  reflection: "How many independent admission decisions sit on your busiest tenant-facing path, and does the last one know who the caller is?",
  recall: {
    q: "Five services on a path each shed 20 percent of load independently. Why is the end-to-end result far worse than 20 percent, and what does DAGOR change?",
    a: "Independent admission probabilities multiply, so end-to-end success is roughly 0.8 to the fifth power, about a third, and every request that fails at a late hop has already consumed capacity at the earlier hops. The wasted work makes the overload worse.\n\nDAGOR assigns business and user priority once at the entry point, propagates it with the request, and has services publish their current admitted priority level back to callers, so the shedding is one coherent decision made early rather than five uncorrelated ones made late.",
  },
  deepDive: "Map the admission decisions on my tenant-facing request path and propose a priority scheme that could be assigned at the gateway and propagated through Temporal activities."
},
{
  id: "saturation-load-shedding",
  track: "saturation", level: "mechanism",
  title: "Shedding load is cheaper than serving it badly",
  source: "David Yanacek, Using load shedding to avoid overload, Amazon Builders' Library",
  gateIntro: "Offered load climbs through the afternoon. Completed requests per second rises, flattens, and then falls, and at peak offered load it is well below the flat portion. CPU is at 100 percent the whole way, so the fleet is certainly doing work. Latency at the point where the curve turns down is longer than the clients' configured timeout.",
  idea: "A server that rejects excess requests immediately holds its goodput flat past the knee, while a server that accepts everything spends its capacity on responses nobody is still waiting for.",
  why: "Yanacek separates throughput from goodput: requests handled versus requests handled correctly and within the caller's deadline. Past the point where latency exceeds the client timeout, every completed request is throughput without goodput. That capacity is not merely wasted, it is subtracted from the requests that could still have succeeded, and the timed-out callers usually retry, so offered load rises as goodput falls. The curve bends downwards rather than flattening, and it does not recover on its own when the input rate stops rising.\n\nRejection works because it is cheap and serving is expensive. A 503 costs a small fraction of a real response, so a server that sheds can hold a flat goodput line under arbitrarily high offered load. The practice is to measure the maximum sustainable rate empirically rather than deriving it, set a limit a little below it, reject above it, and distinguish requests that can be shed from those that cannot. The failure mode to design against is the shedder becoming the bottleneck, which is why the check must happen before the expensive work, ideally before deserialisation, and why health-check and control-plane requests need a reserved allowance so you do not shed your own ability to recover.",
  failureMode: "No shedding, so the fleet degrades uniformly. Readiness probes are requests too, so they time out alongside everything else, Kubernetes restarts pods, capacity drops, and the surviving pods take a larger share of the same offered load. The shedding you refused to design happens anyway, performed by the orchestrator, at the worst possible granularity.",
  experiment: "Load test one service past its knee and plot goodput - responses completed within the client timeout - against offered load, rather than plotting requests per second. Find the offered load at which the goodput curve turns down and note the concurrency at that point. Then set a concurrency limit slightly below it and repeat the test: goodput should stay flat instead of collapsing. Keep both plots.",
  reflection: "For your busiest service, what is the measured concurrency at peak goodput, and what limit is actually configured?",
  recall: {
    q: "A service is at 100 percent CPU and its completed requests per second is falling as offered load rises. Why does adding capacity often fail to fix this, and what is the cheaper intervention?",
    a: "The fleet is spending capacity on requests whose callers have already timed out, and those callers retry, so offered load rises with the extra capacity too. Unless the added capacity exceeds the amplified load, the new servers fill with the same expired work.\n\nThe cheaper intervention is to reject above a measured concurrency limit before doing the expensive work. Rejection costs a fraction of a served response, which is what keeps goodput flat regardless of how high offered load goes.",
  },
  deepDive: "Help me find the peak-goodput concurrency for my heaviest service and design a shedding rule that protects health checks and control-plane calls."
},
{
  id: "saturation-deadline-before-work",
  track: "saturation", level: "mechanism",
  title: "A server should check the deadline before it starts the work",
  source: "David Yanacek, Avoiding insurmountable queue backlogs, Amazon Builders' Library",
  gateIntro: "A queue-backed service accumulates a backlog during a two-hour downstream slowdown. The downstream recovers fully. Workers return to full CPU, the backlog begins draining, and the drain rate matches the normal steady-state processing rate. Client-visible error rates stay at 100 percent for the entire drain, and users who retry immediately get the same result.",
  idea: "Once queue delay exceeds the caller's timeout, every completed unit of work is wasted, so the deadline has to be checked before the work starts rather than discovered after it finishes.",
  why: "A FIFO backlog serves the oldest item first, which under a backlog is precisely the item most likely to have expired. A worker that dequeues an item, spends 200 milliseconds on it, and writes a result nobody is reading has converted capacity into nothing, and the caller who timed out has usually already re-enqueued. The system settles into doing 100 percent of its work for 0 percent of its value, and it does not exit on its own, because the arrival rate of retries is coupled to the completion rate of expired work.\n\nYanacek's remedies are all cheap and all structural. Stamp the caller's deadline onto the item at enqueue and check it before starting, discarding expired items without processing. Prefer LIFO while behind, because when you are behind the newest item is the one whose caller is still waiting, and under normal load LIFO and FIFO are indistinguishable because the queue is near empty. Bound the queue so the backlog cannot exceed the deadline at all. And export the discard count, because a silent discard and a fast success look identical on a throughput graph.",
  failureMode: "A Temporal activity task queue after a spike is forty minutes deep against a thirty-second caller timeout. Workers churn at full rate, every result is thrown away, and the operator sees full CPU and a falling queue depth and reports recovery. The graph that would have shown the truth is the age of items at dequeue, and nobody exports it.",
  experiment: "Pick one queue-backed path and check whether the caller's deadline travels in the message. If not, add it, and log item age at dequeue. Then measure over one busy hour the fraction of items whose age at dequeue already exceeded the client timeout. That fraction is the share of your worker fleet currently producing nothing, and it is the number to put in the incident review.",
  reflection: "Which of your queues carries no deadline, and what would the age-at-dequeue distribution have looked like during your last backlog?",
  recall: {
    q: "A backlog is draining at the normal processing rate but every caller is still getting errors. What is happening, and why does LIFO help while adding workers may not?",
    a: "Queue delay exceeds the client timeout, so each item completed has already been abandoned by its caller, who has retried. The fleet is fully busy producing results nobody reads, and the retries keep the arrival rate up.\n\nLIFO helps because when you are behind, the newest item is the one whose caller is still waiting, so serving it produces value. Adding workers only helps if the added capacity clears the backlog faster than retries refill it; discarding expired items before processing removes the waste directly and is the cheaper move.",
  },
  deepDive: "Show me how to propagate a caller deadline through my Temporal activities and Kafka messages, and what to export so age-at-dequeue is visible during a backlog."
},
{
  id: "saturation-regex-backtracking",
  track: "saturation", level: "mechanism",
  title: "Stack Overflow, 20 July 2016: one post consumed a core through catastrophic backtracking",
  source: "Nick Craver, Outage Postmortem, Stack Exchange, 20 July 2016",
  gateIntro: "At 00:32 UTC on 20 July 2016, Stack Overflow became unavailable. CPU on the web tier saturated, the load balancer removed web servers as they stopped answering health checks, and the site was down for 34 minutes. There had been no deployment, no infrastructure change, and no unusual traffic volume in the minutes before or during the outage.",
  idea: "A request whose cost is unbounded in the content of its input turns a single user into a denial of service, so per-request work limits matter more than per-tenant request limits.",
  why: "The trigger was a post containing roughly twenty thousand consecutive whitespace characters on one line. The pattern used to trim leading and trailing whitespace could match that run in an enormous number of ways, and a backtracking regex engine explores those ways, so a single render of that post occupied a CPU core for a very long time. The post was on the home page, so every home page request paid the cost and the web tier saturated within about a minute. Health checks are ordinary requests, so the load balancer withdrew the servers that were busy, which concentrated the remaining requests on fewer servers.\n\nThe general shape is that rate limits are denominated in requests, and every capacity plan silently assumes bounded cost per request. Anything that breaks that assumption makes request count useless as a load metric: a backtracking regex, unbounded JSON nesting, a query with no LIMIT, a GraphQL query with nested list expansion, a metadata crawl over a table with a hundred thousand columns. The mitigation is a per-request work budget enforced before the work begins - a linear-time regex engine such as RE2, a regex timeout where you must keep backtracking, a maximum input size, a query time and memory cap, a complexity score computed at parse time. Go's regexp package cannot backtrack; Python, Java, .NET and JavaScript all can.",
  failureMode: "A tenant-supplied filter or lineage expression executed against ClickHouse with no per-tenant max_execution_time or max_memory_usage. Request rate stays flat, so the rate limiter never fires, and one query consumes a node's memory. The load never appeared in the metric you built the protection around.",
  experiment: "Find the three request paths where a user controls something whose cost grows superlinearly: regex, recursion depth, result set size, join fan-out, column count. For each, verify a hard limit is enforced before work begins - RE2 or a regex deadline, max_execution_time and max_memory_usage on the query, a depth limit in the parser, a row cap. Write down which of the three currently have none.",
  reflection: "Which tenant-controlled input in your system has no ceiling on the work it can cause, and what is the cheapest ceiling you could add this week?",
  recall: {
    q: "One user's content took down a whole web tier at ordinary traffic volume. Why did per-user rate limiting not protect the site, and what class of control would have?",
    a: "Rate limits bound requests per unit time, which only protects you if cost per request is bounded. Here the cost was a function of the content, so one request could occupy a core indefinitely and the rate limiter saw nothing unusual.\n\nThe control that works is a per-request work budget enforced before or during execution: a linear-time regex engine, a regex or query deadline, an input size cap, or a complexity score computed at parse time and rejected above a threshold.",
  },
  deepDive: "Find the tenant-controlled inputs in my Python and Go services whose processing cost is superlinear, and tell me which regex engines and query paths lack a hard budget."
},
{
  id: "saturation-cfs-throttling",
  track: "saturation", level: "mechanism",
  title: "Kubernetes CPU limits throttle at low average utilisation because quota is enforced every hundred milliseconds",
  source: "Dave Chiluk, Unthrottled: Fixing CPU Limits in the Cloud, Indeed engineering",
  gateIntro: "A Python service in Kubernetes shows p99 latency spikes of several hundred milliseconds, a few times a minute. Container CPU averaged over a minute sits at about a quarter of its configured limit. The node is not busy. No garbage collection pause, network retransmit or downstream slowdown correlates with the spikes, and they continue unchanged after moving the pod to an idle node with the same resource spec.",
  idea: "CFS quota is granted per short period and spent within it, so a bursty service is stopped repeatedly every second while its minute-average utilisation looks comfortable.",
  why: "The Linux CFS bandwidth controller gives a cgroup a quota of CPU time per period. Kubernetes uses a 100 millisecond period by default and derives quota from the CPU limit, so a limit of 0.5 CPU is 50 milliseconds of CPU time per 100 millisecond period. A container that spends its whole quota in the first 20 milliseconds has every runnable thread stopped for the remaining 80, and none of that appears on a one-minute average, which reads 20 percent. The average is arithmetically correct and operationally useless.\n\nThreading amplifies it. Quota is consumed by all threads together, so a Go runtime with GOMAXPROCS set from the node's core count while the container is limited to 2 CPUs can burn a 200 millisecond quota across 16 threads in about 12 milliseconds and then sit stopped for the rest of every period. Setting GOMAXPROCS from the limit, which uber-go/automaxprocs does, removes most of that. There was also a kernel accounting bug that leaked quota into per-CPU slices and throttled containers below their nominal limit; Chiluk's fix landed in Linux 5.4, so older kernels throttle more than the arithmetic predicts. The tell is container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total rising while container_cpu_usage_seconds_total looks unremarkable.",
  failureMode: "A latency SLO breach that survives every application-level investigation, because the application is not slow, it is stopped. Scaling out makes the economics worse rather than better: each new replica gets its own small quota and each still bursts within its period, so you buy more idle reservation and the same throttling.",
  experiment: "In Grafana, plot rate(container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total) divided by rate(container_cpu_cfs_periods_total) per pod over the last day, sorted descending. Anything above a few percent is being stopped. Cross-plot it against CPU usage as a fraction of limit. Pods with high throttling and low usage are the ones where the limit, not the load, is the problem, and for Go services check whether GOMAXPROCS matches the limit or the node.",
  reflection: "Which of your pods are throttled while looking underutilised, and how many of them are Go services taking GOMAXPROCS from the node?",
  recall: {
    q: "A container averages 25 percent of its CPU limit over a minute and still shows repeated latency spikes on an idle node. Explain the mechanism and name the two metrics that confirm it.",
    a: "CFS enforces the limit as a quota inside each 100 millisecond period. A bursty workload exhausts its quota early in the period and is stopped until the next one, which is invisible on a minute average.\n\nConfirm with the ratio of container_cpu_cfs_throttled_periods_total to container_cpu_cfs_periods_total, read alongside container_cpu_usage_seconds_total as a fraction of the limit. High throttling with low usage is the signature. For a Go service also check GOMAXPROCS, since threads spend the shared quota in parallel.",
  },
  deepDive: "Query my Grafana for the pods with the worst throttled-period ratio relative to their CPU usage and tell me which limits to raise and which are a GOMAXPROCS problem."
},
{
  id: "saturation-clickhouse-parts",
  track: "saturation", level: "mechanism",
  title: "ClickHouse too-many-parts is an insert-rate problem, not a disk problem",
  source: "ClickHouse documentation: MergeTree, parts and merges, and asynchronous inserts",
  gateIntro: "An ingest pipeline writing to ClickHouse begins failing on insert. Disk usage across the cluster is under half. CPU is moderate and there is no memory pressure. Reducing the batch size makes the failures more frequent rather than less. Select queries against the same tables have been getting steadily slower for two days before the writes started failing.",
  idea: "Every insert creates a part and merging is a background queue with finite throughput, so inserting faster than the merge scheduler retires parts produces a backlog that eventually rejects writes regardless of free storage.",
  why: "MergeTree writes each insert as an immutable part sorted by the table's ORDER BY, and background merges combine parts into larger ones. Merge throughput is bounded by the merge thread pool and by disk bandwidth, and merging two parts costs work proportional to their combined size, so it is never free. When the rate of part creation exceeds the rate at which the pool retires them, part count per partition climbs, and ClickHouse applies two brakes in sequence: it first delays inserts once part count crosses parts_to_delay_insert, then rejects them with 'Too many parts' at parts_to_throw_insert. Both are counted per partition, so a high-cardinality partition key multiplies the problem because every partition needs its own merges.\n\nThe damage runs in both directions and reads degrade first. A select must read every active part that overlaps its range, so the same backlog that eventually blocks writes has been making queries slower for a while beforehand, which is why the read regression precedes the write failure. The fixes in order of leverage: batch on the client so each insert is large, or enable asynchronous inserts so the server batches for you; reduce partition cardinality so merges are not spread across thousands of partitions; then, and only then, increase the merge pool, which spends IO you may not have. Raising the thresholds is not a fix, it is disabling the brake.",
  failureMode: "Per-tenant partitioning. PARTITION BY (tenant_id, toYYYYMM(ts)) is tidy, makes tenant deletion a cheap DROP PARTITION, and is exactly wrong for ingest: a thousand tenants each writing every few seconds means a thousand partitions each accumulating small parts, none of which can ever be merged with another tenant's. Disk is nearly empty and ingest has stopped.",
  experiment: "Run: SELECT database, table, partition, count() AS parts FROM system.parts WHERE active GROUP BY 1,2,3 ORDER BY parts DESC LIMIT 20. Then read your actual brake settings, since the defaults have changed across versions: SELECT name, value FROM system.merge_tree_settings WHERE name IN ('parts_to_delay_insert','parts_to_throw_insert'). Finally, compare NewPart against MergeParts row counts in system.part_log for the last hour: new parts outnumbering merges is a growing backlog with a date on it.",
  reflection: "Which of your ClickHouse tables has the highest parts-per-partition, and is the cause insert frequency or partition cardinality?",
  recall: {
    q: "ClickHouse is rejecting inserts with 'Too many parts' while disk is half empty. What is the actual constraint, and why does reducing batch size make it worse?",
    a: "The constraint is merge throughput. Each insert creates a part and background merges retire them at a bounded rate; when creation outpaces retirement the per-partition part count crosses the delay and then the throw thresholds, independently of free space.\n\nSmaller batches mean more inserts for the same rows and therefore more parts per second, which raises the creation rate and shortens the time to rejection. The correct direction is fewer, larger inserts, or asynchronous inserts so the server does the batching.",
  },
  deepDive: "Look at my ClickHouse system.parts and part_log and tell me whether my too-many-parts risk is driven by insert frequency, partition cardinality, or merge pool sizing."
},
{
  id: "saturation-universal-scalability",
  track: "saturation", level: "mechanism",
  title: "Past a point, adding workers reduces throughput",
  source: "Neil Gunther, Guerrilla Capacity Planning, 2007 (universal scalability law)",
  gateIntro: "A batch fleet is scaled from 40 workers to 120 to clear a backlog. Throughput rises to about 60 workers, is flat to about 90, and at 120 is measurably below what it was at 60. Per-worker CPU falls as workers are added. The backlog now clears more slowly than it did before the scale-out, and the dashboard still reads as a capacity shortage.",
  idea: "Throughput as a function of concurrency has a maximum, because contention makes scaling sublinear and the cost of keeping state coherent eventually makes it retrograde.",
  why: "Gunther's universal scalability law models relative capacity at N workers as N divided by the quantity one, plus alpha times N minus one, plus beta times N times N minus one. Alpha is contention, the serial fraction, and it flattens the curve. Beta is coherency, the cost of every worker having to agree with every other, and because it carries an N squared term it eventually turns the curve downwards. Amdahl's law is the special case where beta is zero: it flattens and never falls. Any real system with shared mutable state has a non-zero beta, and therefore a peak.\n\nThe practical value is that the peak is measurable rather than theoretical. Fit alpha and beta from three or four load points and the concurrency at maximum throughput falls out, roughly the square root of one minus alpha over beta. If your dashboard says capacity shortage and adding workers makes throughput worse, you are on the far side of that peak, and the correct action is fewer workers or less shared state, not more of either. This is the one mechanism in the track where the intuitive intervention is not merely unhelpful but directly harmful.",
  failureMode: "A worker fleet all polling one Postgres queue table with SKIP LOCKED. Each extra worker adds lock acquisition traffic and row conflict rate, so past a point every worker spends more time contending than working. The same shape appears with a large worker fleet long-polling one Temporal task queue, and with concurrent ClickHouse queries contending for the same mark cache.",
  experiment: "Run the same fixed workload at four concurrency levels - say a quarter, a half, one and two times current - and record throughput at each. Plot throughput against concurrency. If the last point sits below the second-to-last, you have measured a non-zero beta and you are already past the peak. Do this before the next scale-out request, not during the incident when the only available action is to add more.",
  reflection: "Which of your fleets have you never measured below their current size, and what would the four-point curve cost you to produce?",
  recall: {
    q: "Adding workers to a fleet reduced throughput. Which term in the universal scalability law explains a decline rather than a plateau, and what is the corresponding intervention?",
    a: "The coherency term, beta. It carries an N squared cost because every worker must stay consistent with every other, so beyond a point the coordination cost grows faster than the work capacity added and the curve turns down. Contention alone, alpha, would only flatten it.\n\nThe intervention is to remove shared state or partition it so that workers do not have to agree - separate queues, sharded locks, partitioned caches - or simply to run fewer workers at the measured peak.",
  },
  deepDive: "Design a four-point scalability measurement for my Temporal worker fleet and help me fit alpha and beta so I know the concurrency at peak throughput."
},
{
  id: "saturation-shuffle-sharding",
  track: "saturation", level: "mechanism",
  title: "In a shared pool, one tenant's burst is every tenant's latency",
  source: "Colm MacCarthaigh, Workload isolation using shuffle-sharding, Amazon Builders' Library",
  gateIntro: "One tenant on a multi-tenant platform runs an unusual workload for about twenty minutes. During that window error rates and latency rise for every other tenant, and rise by roughly the same amount for each of them. The platform runs eight identical stateless nodes behind a round-robin load balancer. No node failed, none was restarted, and all eight stayed in rotation throughout.",
  idea: "Full sharing means every tenant inherits the worst tenant's behaviour, and assigning each tenant a distinct random subset of capacity reduces the blast radius from everyone to a small fraction.",
  why: "With N nodes and round-robin, a tenant whose workload poisons a node reaches all N of them, so blast radius equals fleet size and grows as you grow. Conventional sharding into fixed groups of size k reduces the radius to one group, but everyone inside that group is fully exposed and there are only N over k groups, so the chance of sharing a group with a heavy neighbour stays high. Shuffle sharding assigns each tenant a random subset of k nodes from N, which gives N choose k possible shards instead. With 8 nodes and shards of 2 there are 28; with 100 nodes and shards of 5 there are over 75 million. The probability that another tenant occupies your entire shard becomes small enough to compute rather than fear.\n\nThe property that does the work is overlap, not isolation. Two tenants will often share one node, so a bad workload still degrades some fraction of everyone's requests, and the scheme only pays off if the client retries onto another node in its own shard. That means shuffle sharding is a joint design between the routing layer and the retry policy, not a routing trick. The construction applies to worker pools, connection pools, queue partitions and cache instances just as much as to endpoints.",
  failureMode: "One Temporal task queue and one worker deployment serving all tenants. Cheap, simple, and correct right up until the first heavy tenant, after which every other tenant's latency is their problem too. The only mitigation available during the incident is to identify and pause the offending tenant, which reliably takes longer than the incident should have lasted.",
  experiment: "Count the shared resources on one request path that every tenant touches: worker deployments, task queues, connection pools, cache instances. For each, write what a single tenant can do to make it unusable. Then compute what shuffle sharding would buy at your current node count N with a shard of 2 and of 3: the number of distinct shards is N choose k, and the chance a given other tenant fully overlaps you is one over that. Compare with your current blast radius of one.",
  reflection: "Which shared resource in your estate has a blast radius of all tenants, and what is the smallest shard size that would give you a computable overlap probability?",
  recall: {
    q: "Why does shuffle sharding reduce blast radius far more than dividing a fleet into fixed shards of the same size, and what must the client do for it to work?",
    a: "Fixed sharding gives N over k groups; shuffle sharding gives N choose k possible subsets, which is dramatically larger, so the chance that another tenant occupies your entire subset becomes very small even though pairwise overlap on one node stays common.\n\nIt only works if the client retries onto another node within its own shard. Without retries, partial overlap means a bad neighbour still degrades a share of every request, and the combinatorial advantage is never realised.",
  },
  deepDive: "Tell me where shuffle sharding would apply in my multi-tenant Kubernetes and Temporal setup, and what shard size gives a sensible overlap probability at my tenant count."
}
);
